        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new validation lives in the right unit \u2014 `OwnerTelephones.toE164`, the single shared definition that both callers use \u2014 so placement and non-duplication are correct. But the added block itself is not idiomatic and would read awkwardly to a maintainer:\n\n- **Redundant nesting.** `if (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) { if (...) return null; }` uses two nested `if`s where one compound condition is the natural form here. Each branch reads more clearly as a single guard, e.g. `if (digits.startsWith(\"61\") && digits.length() != 11) return null;` and `else if (digits.startsWith(\"1\") && digits.length() != 11) return null;`. The nesting adds indentation without expressing anything the `&&` doesn't.\n\n- **`\"61\".length()` / `\"1\".length()` arithmetic obscures a constant.** `digits.length() - \"61\".length() != 9` is a roundabout way to say the total must be 11 digits. Calling `.length()` on a string literal to recover a number the reader already knows (2, 1) hurts readability; use the literal directly (`digits.length() != 11`) or a named constant.\n\n- **Comment describes a safeguard the code doesn't contain.** The comment says country codes are \"matched longest-first so '+61' isn't mistaken for '+6'.\" There is no `'+6'` branch, and because a string starting `\"61\"` never satisfies `startsWith(\"1\")`, the 61-before-1 ordering carries no significance. The comment invites a maintainer to preserve an ordering invariant that isn't real; either add the case it references or drop the claim.\n\n- **Shape doesn't scale.** Two countries as an if/else chain is acceptable now, but the structure (`startsWith` + hardcoded expected length per branch) will grow into a special-case ladder as countries are added. If more than these two are anticipated, a small `code \u2192 nationalLength` lookup would be the more natural home for this rule. Not required for two, but worth noting given the comment already frames this as an extensible per-country policy."
